YAAP bourland-Wiz-Gno-Mal-Neu (foodless atheist genocideless
polypileless survivor)


1. Background

I started playing nethack 3.3.0 back in March 2000, I played off and
on until I ascended for the first time that October in interesting
fashion.  With then roommate Jason Short looking over my shoulder, I
feared what may lie beyond that starting <, and made sure the coast
was clear of all creatures.  Thus was coined the phrase
"extinctionist".  As is a common story, the game still had an allure,
because I wanted to see what a populated Planes was like.  Playing
only random characters (as always) I eventually got a few wombats
close before knocking B off the list.  All was well until I moved
across town, and without that nethack server, my playing days were on
hold.

After a two year hiatus I was back and playing, and started lurking
around here from time to time, and was shocked to see how conduct
crazy everyone had gotten.  While I really enjoy the simplicity of
playing and caring for the survival of the random characters
generated, I started dwelling on some conduct possibilities.  I read
some prior posts and got some general plans, but didn't want to study
too in depth, but rather mess up on my own.  I started out most with
Eleven Wizards (for starting equipment) and even a few Monks (for
intrinsics), but most met their end with starvation.  I never got the
needed starting ring, but got good at minimizing turn usage.  That
same day, after failing a few Monks again I tried random Wizards and
in that endeavor I found the one.  Below is my trial of a foodless
atheist, and the thrill of victory with death only a few turns away at
the end.

2. Surviving Starvation - my little conduct game (foodless atheist
genocideless polypileless survivor)

Bourland, the Wiz-Gno-Mal-Neu along with his sidekick cat Azrael,
started out his adventure with:
?oEW, ?oGD, ?oMM, +oFB, +oClair, !oOD, !oWater, !oSleeping, =oTel,
=oProt, /oLight

Using ?oMM first, he rushed to the downstairs and at T349 found a
quaint little / shop below.  Not the richest man, he did some window
shopping and took some notes, but his pants turned brown at the sight
of the 888 zorkmid price tag on one.  A little overanxious, some
unpaid merchandise was fired at the owner, and the Shopkeeper yelled
"Hey, you gotta pay for that zap - $222, pronto!".  A little light in
the wallet, but grinning at the shiny new =oSD, he dropped and sold
all the owner would buy and raised funds up to $237 to cover the cost.

Since the cat had life easy with this owner who let him get as fat as
he wanted, he snuck the used wand past and off the pair wore, safely
knowing that starvation was a mere 8000 turns or so away.

So back to the top they went and explored (hastily mind you) and found
some pet treats and an uncursed amulet.  Some more wands were borrowed
from the original shop, but with the turn counter on the rise, deeper
they descended.

On a roll, Bourland found GoP on Dlvl4 (T2042) and could now safely
kill things without as much fear.  Dlvl5 brought suspicious activity
from a book shopkeeper, so Bourland thought it time to lower that AC
of 9, and zapped his way to 2b?oCharging, +3 GreenDSM and the new AC
was -3.  This proved wise, as a large mimic gave a mild scare in the
+store which had scrolls to price, of which the cheapest one was
bought and used to ID the "oLS found on Dlvl1, as well as his most
used spellbook, Magic Missile.

Compared to his friends who went down the failed foodless atheist
road, Bourland was sitting pretty around T3000 with poison resistance,
MR, a most useful attack spell, and a wand burning to be used in the
back of his pocket.

A little too patiently exploring, the turn counter was ticking away,
and so some speed boots were zapped up (blessed fixed +2) to make time
more efficient.  He and Azrael entered Sokoban at T4093, and
immediately noticed the nutritional mistake of wearing the neck
jewelry since identification.  The bottom yielded three great treats:
the "oR, a lock pick, and the pleasure of poison resistance making the
4 bees not a factor.

Bourland the Thaumaturge  St:25 Dx:11 Co:17 In:19 Wi:11 Ch:10  Neutral
Dlvl:7  $:1883 HP:59(59) Pw:42(67) AC:-6 Xp:8/2472 T:6352 Hungry

Hunger settled in earlier than expected.  The combination of 324 turns
without a =oSD and the 3500 of wearing an amulet did a number on the
nutrition status.  A decision on how to deal with this could be
delayed, but probably only 3K more turns he anticipated.

T7340 he entered the mines and found a bag (not holding), a white
unicorn which selflessly yielded it's horn, and a leash.  Minetown
yielded more price IDing, as well as great finds of a magic lamp (yay
lightsource) and a tinning kit.  Without means to improve luck, the
luckstone could wait, so a stash was formed for the extra junk (a
common theme), and back to the Dungeons of Doom for the party of three
- now with the addition of a tame kitten named Brunhilda.

T8286 Hunger went to Weak, and so the party sat around a locked room
and told stories over a handful of potions of ale, and he was weak no
more.  The hangover set in quickly, as at T9517 he was too hungry to
cast spells, and T9681 ("oLS back on) started Fainting spells.

Not yet wanting to take Marvin's polyself road, nor the "oLS route,
and wished away for the PYEC and a blessed Horn of Plenty.  Never
before has the Turn counter been so carefully tracked for the #invoke
command, and soon enough food for an army was emptied onto the floor,
as was as the great find of holy water and the occasional gin and
juice.  The process continued, but after 10 or so invocations it was a
losing cause.  Had the strategy been implemented from the WoW find, or
had he been a Tourist with blessed charging then I *think* it could
work, but one potion every 2 or 3 invocations is not a way to get by.

At the brink of starvation,, he settled into a semi-safe spot and
between wresting and fainting the Marvin route won out, as a =oPC
appeared with the final wish.  Removing the /oPolymorph found
somewhere after Minetown, he prepared for the nutrition charge and
zap, "Become what kind of monster? [type the name]"

Unlike the 15 or so failed Eleven Wizard before him, he was finally
ready for the big change, and with excitement he yelled out, elf!.  A
booming voice laughed out, "You cannot polymorph into that."  Um but
but but...I wanna be a new Elf.  Tries again - Elf, same response. 
ELF!!  (why isn't this working?) Elf!???  (how can I safely get out of
this prompt?).  Then it hits me...hmm, maybe i'm not an Elf - oh wait
- I hit * after choosing Wizard and have no idea what race I am.  In
fact, I'm Neutral, and being an Eleven Wizard wouldn't allow for that.
 Scanning through the race/class combos available, I was certain I
must be human.  Ok, so on the 5th try:

"Become what kind of monster? [type the name]" human
"You cannot polymorph into that.  That's enough tries!"
"You rip out of your cloak"
"You merge with your scaly armor"

So i guess i'm a gnome ;)  How did I miss that?  So, lost my cloak,
lost the polyself conduct, but at least knew what race I now was.  One
more zap and I was a happy new Gnome, and hunger was a problem no more
again, and with careful use of the Horn of Plenty i would delay the
next problem.

3. Mid Game

So onward he charged, now with a magic whistle found on the body of a
wolf, and a new camp was set at in the big room.  A few big battles
here tough in treasure zoos, and an even tougher one in a Dlvl20
throne room, so at this point retreat/regrouping was wise since none
of his wand charges were known.  Having not found ?oID since they
early two, much of his stash was only price IDed.

Also a problem was a lack of Magic Cancellation, which was remarkable
that no cloak had been found since his cloak of MR ripped.  Off to
safer ground, he went to the mines for the luckstone, and to his
pleasant surprise a gray unicorn was waiting at the bottom.  With luck
now on his side, it seemed to pay off by finding a blindfold
(finally!), a stethoscope, and a robe (minetown priest).  The RNG
chose to mock him also by placing another Horn of Plenty sitting
around the mines.

Back to the main dungeon, potions brought me to lvl14 so the Dark One
was next on the hit list.  The quest brought a few scrolls of identify
and useful spells learned (healing, remove curse).  Nutrition took a
random turn for the worse while dealing with the dark one, as mid
battle hunger went from Hungry to Weak, and 10 turns after offing the
Dark One, to Weak.  Naturally, his =oSD had somehow been removed in my
haste.  Ooops!

*recharge*

